  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Serven's two-run homer in the ninth fell one swing short of forcing extras. Mason Barnett cracked after 4.1 innings, and the bullpen. Alvarado, Basso, Johnson. Kept it tight enough to matter, but not tight enough to win. Cincinnati held on 6\u20135, and the Athletics fell another half-game back in a division they're already chasing from deep water. This is what happens when chances convert late but not late enough.",
    "what_this_means": "The storyline was the same as yesterday and the day before: early deficit, mid-game scramble, ninth-inning rally that landed just short. They scored three in the fourth to take a two-run lead, then watched Cincinnati punch back twice in the fifth and hold on. Serven drove in two with his homer. The lineup's only real impact after a half-dozen hits went nowhere. This is inconsistency wearing a familiar mask. You get runners on. You don't finish the conversion. Eventually, not finishing becomes the thing that defines you. The margin keeps shrinking. Now 13.5 games back. But so does the time.",
    "what_to_watch": "Boston comes to Oakland tonight with Payton Tolle on the mound, a 3.30 ERA that the Athletics lineup has not seen much lately. Jack Perkins takes the ball for Oakland.",
